unspecified","delay-in-days":0,"URL":"https:\/\/creativecommons.org\/licenses\/by\/4.0"}],"content-domain":{"domain":[],"crossmark-restriction":false},"short-container-title":["ACGN Nex."],"abstract":"<jats:p>ggDragonMaid is an R package that provides classically themed color palettes derived from the twelve principal characters of the anime series Miss Kobayashi\u2019s Dragon Maid, along with a multilingual character information dataset (Chinese, Japanese, and Romaji). The package enables users to integrate these visually distinctive palettes into ggplot2 and base R graphics, offering a playful yet practical tool for data visualization. A built-in language detection function automatically identifies the input language, allowing users to query palettes and character data using names in any of the three supported languages. The package also includes CP (couple) color mapping, character search capabilities, and illustrative example plots. This paper presents the design philosophy, core functionalities, implementation details, and use cases of ggDragonMaid, positioning it within the broader ecosystem of pop-culture-inspired color palette packages for R.<\/jats:p>","DOI":"10.66309\/7q3ekt83","type":"journal-article","created":{"date-parts":[[2026,5,22]],"date-time":"2026-05-22T12:56:39Z","timestamp":1779454599000},"page":"e0007","source":"Crossref","is-referenced-by-count":0,"title":["ggDragonMaid: An R Package for Anime-Inspired Color Palettes and Multilingual Character Data from Miss Kobayashi\u2019s Dragon Maid"],"prefix":"10.66309","volume":"1","author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0007-3615-5724","authenticated-orcid":true,"given":"Zhaoshuo","family":"Liu","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"id":[{"id":"https:\/\/ror.org\/03angcq70","id-type":"ROR","asserted-by":"publisher"}],"name":"University of Birmingham"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"member":"55911","published-online":{"date-parts":[[2026,5,22]]},"container-title":["ACGN Nexus"],"link":[{"URL":"https:\/\/journal.scihorizon.org\/index.php\/ACGN\/article\/download\/22\/18","content-type":"application\/pdf","content-version":"vor","intended-application":"text-mining"},{"URL":"https:\/\/journal.scihorizon.org\/index.php\/ACGN\/article\/download\/22\/18","content-type":"unspecified","content-version":"vor","intended-application":"similarity-checking"}],"deposited":{"date-parts":[[2026,5,22]],"date-time":"2026-05-22T12:56:39Z","timestamp":1779454599000},"score":0.0,"resource":{"primary":{"URL":"https:\/\/journal.scihorizon.org\/index.php\/ACGN\/article\/view\/22"}},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2026,5,22]]},"references-count":0,"journal-issue":{"issue":"1","published-online":{"date-parts":[[2026,12,31]]}},"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.66309\/7q3ekt83","ISSN":["3142-8452"],"issn-type":[{"value":"3142-8452","type":"electronic"}],"published":{"date-parts":[[2026,5,22]]}},{"indexed":{"date-parts":[[2026,5,5]],"date-time":"2026-05-05T03:48:21Z","timestamp":1777952901036,"version":"3.51.4"},"reference-count":0,"publisher":"SciHorizon Global LLC","issue":"1","license":[{"start":{"date-parts":[[2026,3,23]],"date-time":"2026-03-23T00:00:00Z","timestamp":1774224000000},"content-version":"unspecified","delay-in-days":0,"URL":"https:\/\/creativecommons.org\/licenses\/by\/4.0"}],"content-domain":{"domain":[],"crossmark-restriction":false},"short-container-title":["ACGN Nex."],"abstract":"<jats:p>Drawing on Winnicott's theory of transitional space, this study examines how tabletop role-playing games (TRPGs) provide a unique psychological space for adults confronting developmental challenges. Taking Call of Cthulhu (COC) as an extreme case, and drawing on analysis of its rulebook and community practices, this study identifies a three-tier structure within the TRPG psychological mechanism. First, the Game Master (GM), ritualized community contracts (e.g., Session 0), and rule design together constitute a stable and predictable holding environment. Second, role character sheets and dice function as transitional objects, enabling players to engage in emotional projection, identity experimentation, and narrative reconstruction. Third, COC's unique Sanity (SAN) mechanism gamifies the primal anxiety of \"self-disintegration,\" thereby offering an extreme test case for examining the boundaries of transitional space. This study suggests that TRPGs, through their transitional psychological boundaries, enable adults to experience extreme psychological situations within the safety of structured rules, thereby repairing and rebuilding their capacity for play and restoring a balance between reality and fantasy.<\/jats:p>","DOI":"10.66309\/z956h892","type":"journal-article","created":{"date-parts":[[2026,5,4]],"date-time":"2026-05-04T13:25:42Z","timestamp":1777901142000},"page":"e0004","source":"Crossref","is-referenced-by-count":0,"title":["\u4ece\u5c0f\u6bef\u5b50\u5230\u9ab0\u5b50\uff1a\u6210\u4eba\u5982\u4f55\u5728\u684c\u6e38\u4e2d\u91cd\u65b0\u5b66\u4f1a\u6e38\u620f\u2014\u2014\u4ee5\u300a\u514b\u82cf\u9c81\u7684\u547c\u5524\u300b\u4e3a\u4f8b\u7684\u8fc7\u6e21\u6027\u7a7a\u95f4\u5206\u6790","From Blankets to Dice: How Adults Relearn to Play at the Game Table\u2014\u2014A Transitional Space Analysis Based on Call of Cthulhu"],"prefix":"10.66309","volume":"1","author":[{"family":"Fan","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"member":"55911","published-online":{"date-parts":[[2026,3,23]]},"container-title":["ACGN Nexus"],"link":[{"URL":"https:\/\/journal.scihorizon.org\/index.php\/ACGN\/article\/download\/11\/15","content-type":"application\/pdf","content-version":"vor","intended-application":"text-mining"},{"URL":"https:\/\/journal.scihorizon.org\/index.php\/ACGN\/article\/download\/11\/15","content-type":"unspecified","content-version":"vor","intended-application":"similarity-checking"}],"deposited":{"date-parts":[[2026,5,4]],"date-time":"2026-05-04T13:25:42Z","timestamp":1777901142000